   'rule_ref',
   '{"rule":"directoryListing"}'::jsonb,
   'free',
   '{"summary":"Disable directory autoindex on your web server / host.","steps":["Turn off autoindex/directory listing at the server or CDN level.","Serve an index file or return 403/404 for directory paths.","Move private files (uploads, backups) out of the public web root."]}'::jsonb,
   'My site has directory listing enabled on one or more folders. Disable autoindex so directories don''t list their contents, and move any private files out of the public web root. Show me the config.',
   true),
  ('dom-eval-xss', 'generic', 'Cross-site scripting', 'high',
   'DOM XSS via eval() on user input',
   'The shipped JavaScript passes DOM input (getElementById/querySelector .value) directly to eval() or new Function(). Any attacker-controlled string executes as code in the victim''s browser.',
   'User input is executed via eval() — full session compromise via crafted input.',
   'rule_ref',
   '{"rule":"domEvalXss"}'::jsonb,
   'free',
   '{"summary":"Remove eval() and new Function() on any user-influenced string.","steps":["Delete eval()/new Function() calls that read from DOM inputs.","Parse structured data with JSON.parse and validate with a schema.","Render user content as text, never as executable code."]}'::jsonb,
   'My client bundle calls eval() or new Function() on DOM input (.value from getElementById/querySelector). Remove dynamic code execution, parse input safely, and show the fixed handler.',
   true),
  ('domgo-ajax-dom-xss-evidence', 'generic', 'Cross-site scripting', 'high',
   'DomGoat AJAX response DOM XSS exercise disclosed',
   'DomGoat /cxss/example pages fetch JSON via XHR and assign response payloads to msgboard.innerHTML — deliberate AJAX-driven DOM XSS labs.',
   'AJAX-to-innerHTML flows enable DOM XSS when server responses include attacker-controlled strings rendered unsafely.',
   'rule_ref',
   '{"rule":"domgoAjaxDomXssEvidence"}'::jsonb,
   'deep',
   '{"summary":"Never render AJAX/JSON response fields into innerHTML; use textContent or strict sanitization.","steps":["Use textContent instead of innerHTML for XHR/fetch response data.","Validate and encode JSON fields before DOM insertion.","Host DomGoat-style labs on isolated training infrastructure."]}'::jsonb,
   'Our DomGoat exercise writes XHR JSON response payloads into msgboard.innerHTML. Show safe textContent alternatives.',
   true),
  ('domgo-hash-dom-xss-evidence', 'generic', 'Cross-site scripting', 'high',
   'DomGoat hash-fragment DOM XSS exercise disclosed',
   'DomGoat /cxss/example pages assign location.hash fragments to msgboard.innerHTML — deliberate hash-based DOM XSS labs.',
   'Hash-to-innerHTML flows enable DOM XSS when attackers craft malicious URL fragments victims open.',
   'rule_ref',
   '{"rule":"domgoHashDomXssEvidence"}'::jsonb,
   'deep',
   '{"summary":"Never render location.hash into innerHTML; use textContent or strict sanitization.","steps":["Use textContent instead of innerHTML for hash-derived strings.","Validate and encode hash fragments before DOM insertion.","Host DomGoat-style labs on isolated training infrastructure."]}'::jsonb,
   'Our DomGoat exercise writes location.hash into msgboard.innerHTML. Show safe textContent alternatives.',
   true),
  ('domgo-hash-sanitization-bypass-dom-xss-evidence', 'generic', 'Cross-site scripting', 'high',
   'DomGoat hash sanitization-bypass DOM XSS exercise disclosed',
   'DomGoat /cxss/example pages unescape location.hash then apply weak replace() filters before msgboard.innerHTML — deliberate sanitization-bypass DOM XSS labs.',
   'Weak hash sanitization before innerHTML still enables DOM XSS when filters miss attack vectors.',
   'rule_ref',
   '{"rule":"domgoHashSanitizationBypassDomXssEvidence"}'::jsonb,
   'deep',
   '{"summary":"Never rely on partial replace() sanitization before innerHTML; use textContent or a strict HTML sanitizer.","steps":["Use textContent instead of innerHTML for hash-derived strings.","Apply allowlist encoding rather than blacklist replace() filters.","Host DomGoat-style labs on isolated training infrastructure."]}'::jsonb,
   'Our DomGoat exercise unescapes location.hash with weak replace() then writes to msgboard.innerHTML. Show safe encoding alternatives.',
   true),
  ('domgo-localstorage-dom-xss-evidence', 'generic', 'Cross-site scripting', 'high',
   'DomGoat localStorage DOM XSS exercise disclosed',
   'DomGoat /cxss/example pages read localStorage payloads into msgboard.innerHTML — deliberate client-storage DOM XSS labs.',
   'localStorage-to-innerHTML flows enable stored DOM XSS when poisoned values are rendered unsafely.',
   'rule_ref',
   '{"rule":"domgoLocalStorageDomXssEvidence"}'::jsonb,
   'deep',
   '{"summary":"Never render localStorage values into innerHTML; treat stored client data as untrusted input.","steps":["Use textContent instead of innerHTML for localStorage-derived strings.","Sanitize stored payloads before DOM insertion.","Host DomGoat-style labs on isolated training infrastructure."]}'::jsonb,
   'Our DomGoat exercise reads localStorage into msgboard.innerHTML. Show safe rendering and input validation.',
   true),
  ('domgo-postmessage-dom-xss-evidence', 'generic', 'Cross-site scripting', 'high',
   'DomGoat postMessage DOM XSS exercise disclosed',
   'DomGoat /cxss/example pages assign postMessage event data to msgboard.innerHTML — deliberate cross-window DOM XSS labs.',
   'postMessage-to-innerHTML flows enable DOM XSS when pages accept untrusted message payloads.',
   'rule_ref',
   '{"rule":"domgoPostMessageDomXssEvidence"}'::jsonb,
   'deep',
   '{"summary":"Validate postMessage origin and never render evt.data into innerHTML without sanitization.","steps":["Check event.origin against an allowlist before processing postMessage data.","Use textContent instead of innerHTML for message payloads.","Host DomGoat-style labs on isolated training infrastructure."]}'::jsonb,
   'Our DomGoat exercise writes postMessage evt.data into msgboard.innerHTML. Show origin validation and safe rendering.',
   true),
  ('domgo-query-param-dom-xss-evidence', 'generic', 'Cross-site scripting', 'high',
   'DomGoat URL query-parameter DOM XSS exercise disclosed',
   'DomGoat /cxss/example pages parse location.href query strings and window.name into msgboard.innerHTML — deliberate URL-parameter DOM XSS labs.',
   'Query-parameter-to-innerHTML flows enable DOM XSS when URL parameters are rendered without encoding.',
   'rule_ref',
   '{"rule":"domgoQueryParamDomXssEvidence"}'::jsonb,
   'deep',
   '{"summary":"Never render location.href query parameters into innerHTML; use textContent or strict encoding.","steps":["Use textContent instead of innerHTML for URL-derived strings.","Validate query parameter names and values against an allowlist.","Host DomGoat-style labs on isolated training infrastructure."]}'::jsonb,
   'Our DomGoat exercise parses location.href query parameters into msgboard.innerHTML. Show safe encoding alternatives.',
   true),
  ('domgo-referrer-dom-xss-evidence', 'generic', 'Cross-site scripting', 'high',
   'DomGoat referrer-driven DOM XSS exercise disclosed',
   'DomGoat /cxss/example pages assign document.referrer query parameters to msgboard.innerHTML — a deliberate referrer-based DOM XSS lab.',
   'Referrer-to-innerHTML flows enable DOM XSS when attackers control the Referer header on victim navigation.',
   'rule_ref',
   '{"rule":"domgoReferrerDomXssEvidence"}'::jsonb,
   'deep',
   '{"summary":"Never render document.referrer into innerHTML; sanitize or ignore referrer data in DOM sinks.","steps":["Use textContent instead of innerHTML for referrer-derived strings.","Validate referrer URLs against an allowlist before display.","Host DomGoat-style labs on isolated training infrastructure."]}'::jsonb,
   'Our DomGoat exercise writes document.referrer into msgboard.innerHTML. Show safe alternatives and referrer validation.',
   true),
  ('domgo-sinks-catalog-dom-xss-evidence', 'generic', 'Cross-site scripting', 'high',
   'DomGoat XSS sinks catalog with eval/innerHTML/document.write demos',
   'DomGoat /cxss/sinks page catalogs interactive eval(), innerHTML, document.write, and document.writeln XSS sink demonstrations.',
   'Public sink catalogs teach attackers which DOM APIs to target for code execution in client-side apps.',
   'rule_ref',
   '{"rule":"domgoSinksCatalogDomXssEvidence"}'::jsonb,
   'deep',
   '{"summary":"Never expose interactive XSS sink playgrounds on production domains.","steps":["Use textContent instead of innerHTML for untrusted strings.","Avoid eval(), document.write, and document.writeln with user-controlled input.","Host DomGoat-style labs on isolated training infrastructure."]}'::jsonb,
   'Our DomGoat /cxss/sinks page demos eval() and innerHTML sinks. Show safe alternatives and lab isolation.',
   true),
  ('domgo-websocket-dom-xss-evidence', 'generic', 'Cross-site scripting', 'high',
   'DomGoat WebSocket message DOM XSS exercise disclosed',
   'DomGoat /cxss/example pages assign WebSocket onmessage payloads to msgboard.innerHTML — deliberate real-time DOM XSS labs.',
   'WebSocket-to-innerHTML flows enable DOM XSS when message payloads are rendered without validation.',
   'rule_ref',
   '{"rule":"domgoWebsocketDomXssEvidence"}'::jsonb,
   'deep',
   '{"summary":"Validate WebSocket message payloads and never render them into innerHTML without sanitization.","steps":["Authenticate and validate WebSocket message schema before processing.","Use textContent instead of innerHTML for WebSocket payloads.","Host DomGoat-style labs on isolated training infrastructure."]}'::jsonb,
   'Our DomGoat exercise writes WebSocket message payloads into msgboard.innerHTML. Show safe rendering and message validation.',
   true),
  ('domgo-window-name-dom-xss-evidence', 'generic', 'Cross-site scripting', 'high',
   'DomGoat window.name + hash DOM XSS exercise disclosed',
   'DomGoat /cxss/example pages concatenate location.hash and window.name into msgboard.innerHTML — deliberate multi-source DOM XSS labs.',
   'Combining window.name and hash fragments into innerHTML enables DOM XSS via cross-page name poisoning.',
   'rule_ref',
   '{"rule":"domgoWindowNameDomXssEvidence"}'::jsonb,
   'deep',
   '{"summary":"Never render window.name or hash fragments into innerHTML; treat both as untrusted attacker input.","steps":["Use textContent instead of innerHTML for window.name and hash-derived strings.","Clear window.name on navigation and avoid reading it for DOM rendering.","Host DomGoat-style labs on isolated training infrastructure."]}'::jsonb,
   'Our DomGoat exercise concatenates location.hash and window.name into msgboard.innerHTML. Show safe rendering alternatives.',
   true),
  ('exposed-config-backup', 'generic', 'Exposed secret', 'critical',
   'Config or backup file reachable over HTTP',
   'A configuration, credential, or database-backup file is served directly from the web root. AI deploy scaffolds routinely leave these in the published artifact, exposing connection strings, API keys, and full data dumps.',
   'A config/backup file with secrets or data is downloadable by anyone.',
   'http_probe',
   '{"paths":["/.env.development","/.env.backup","/config.json","/appsettings.json","/credentials.json","/.npmrc","/docker-compose.yml","/database.sql","/dump.sql","/backup.sql","/db.sqlite","/.DS_Store"],"expect":{"kind":"body_regex","regex":"(=|:|\\bBEGIN\\b|INSERT INTO|CREATE TABLE|\"[a-z_]+\"\\s*:)"},"maxBytes":4096}'::jsonb,
   'free',
   '{"summary":"Stop serving config/backup files from the web root and rotate anything they exposed.","steps":["Remove config, credential, and *.sql/backup files from the deployed artifact and web root.","Add host deny rules for dotfiles and *.sql / *.sqlite / *.json config paths.","Rotate every credential any exposed file contained."]}'::jsonb,
   'My deployed site serves config/backup files (e.g. config.json, dump.sql, .env.*) over HTTP. Ensure these are never included in the deployed web root, add deny rules for them, and list which secrets I must rotate.',
   true),
  ('exposed-env-file', 'generic', 'Exposed secret', 'critical',
   'Environment file reachable over HTTP',
   'A .env file is served by the production deploy. These files hold database URLs, API keys and signing secrets. AI scaffolds frequently leave them inside the deployed web root.',
   'Your .env file (all your secret keys) is downloadable by anyone.',
   'http_probe',
   '{"paths":["/.env","/.env.local","/.env.production","/api/.env"],"expect":{"kind":"body_regex","regex":"[A-Z0-9_]+="},"maxBytes":4096}'::jsonb,
   'free',
   '{"summary":"Stop serving .env and rotate every secret it contained.","steps":["Move .env outside the deployed web root and add it to your ignore/deny rules.","Rotate every credential the file contained.","Add a deny rule for dotfiles in your host config."]}'::jsonb,
   'My deployed site serves my .env file over HTTP. Stop it from being publicly served, ensure .env is excluded from the deploy/web root, and list every secret it contained so I can rotate them.',
   true),
  ('exposed-git-dir', 'generic', 'Source exposure', 'high',
   'Exposed .git directory',
   'The .git directory is reachable, which lets anyone reconstruct your full source history including any secrets committed along the way.',
   'Your full source code and its history can be downloaded.',
   'http_probe',
   '{"_raw":"{ paths: [\"/.git/HEAD\", \"/.git/config\"], expect: { kind: \"body_regex\", regex: \"ref:|\\\\[core\\\\]\" }, maxBytes: 2048 }"}'::jsonb,
   'free',
   '{"summary":"Stop serving the .git directory from your production host.","steps":["Exclude .git from the deployed artifact / web root.","Add a host rule denying access to /.git.","Audit history for any committed secrets and rotate them."]}'::jsonb,
   'My production deploy exposes the /.git directory. Make sure .git is never deployed or served, add a deny rule for it, and check whether any secrets were committed to history.',
   true),
  ('exposed-source-maps', 'generic', 'Source exposure', 'medium',
   'Source maps published to production',
   'JavaScript source maps are served in production, exposing your original (pre-minified) source. The default AI builder template often does not strip them.',
   'Your original source code is readable in the browser.',
   'rule_ref',
   '{"rule":"sourceMaps"}'::jsonb,
   'free',
   '{"summary":"Disable source map emission for production builds.","steps":["Turn off productionBrowserSourceMaps / build.sourcemap in your config.","Re-deploy and confirm .map files 404."]}'::jsonb,
   'My production build publishes JavaScript source maps. Disable production source maps in the build config and confirm the .map files are no longer served.',
   true),
  ('firebase-config-exposed', 'firebase', 'Information exposure', 'info',
   'Firebase web config present in bundle',
   'The Firebase web config (apiKey, projectId, etc.) is intentionally public, but its presence tells an attacker to test your security rules. This is informational unless rules are open.',
   'Reveals your Firebase project so attackers can probe its rules. Safe only if rules are locked down.',
   'secret_regex',
   '{"regex":"AIza[0-9A-Za-z_\\-]{35}","secretName":"Firebase web API key"}'::jsonb,
   'free',
   null,
   '',
   true),
  ('firebase-open-firestore-rules', 'firebase', 'Broken access control', 'critical',
   'Firestore / Realtime DB rules left open',
   'AI builders commonly ship the Firebase quickstart rules `allow read, write: if true;`. Combined with the public web config, anyone can read and write your entire database.',
   'Anyone on the internet can read and overwrite your database.',
   'rule_ref',
   '{"rule":"firebaseRules","options":{"harmlessProbe":true}}'::jsonb,
   'free',
   '{"summary":"Replace permissive rules with auth- and ownership-scoped rules.","steps":["Open the Firebase console > Firestore/Realtime Database > Rules.","Remove any `if true` allow rule.","Require request.auth != null and scope documents to request.auth.uid."]}'::jsonb,
   'My Firebase security rules are open (allow read, write: if true). Rewrite the Firestore (and Realtime Database, if present) rules so reads/writes require authentication and are scoped to the document owner via request.auth.uid. Output the complete rules file.',
   true),
  ('graphql-endpoint-disclosed-in-docs', 'generic', 'Information disclosure', 'medium',
   'GraphQL server URL disclosed in API documentation',
   'SpectaQL or Swagger-style API docs embed the GraphQL server URL (often localhost:PORT/graphql), exposing the full schema, queries, and mutations to anyone who can read the docs page.',
   'GraphQL schema and mutation catalog are readable from public API docs.',
   'rule_ref',
   '{"rule":"graphqlEndpointDisclosedInDocs"}'::jsonb,
   'free',
   '{"summary":"Remove internal GraphQL URLs from public docs and gate API reference behind auth.","steps":["Do not publish SpectaQL/Swagger docs with localhost or internal GraphQL URLs on public hosts.","Require authentication for API reference pages in production.","Disable GraphQL playground and introspection on any reachable GraphQL endpoint."]}'::jsonb,
   'Our SpectaQL docs expose a GraphQL server URL and full schema on a public page. Show how to redact internal endpoints from docs and lock down the live GraphQL API.',
   true),
  ('graphql-introspection-enabled', 'generic', 'Information disclosure', 'medium',
   'GraphQL introspection is enabled in production',
   'A public GraphQL endpoint answers introspection queries, handing attackers a complete map of your schema — every type, query, and mutation, including internal ones. Auto-generated GraphQL layers (Hasura, Apollo, Postgraphile) ship with it on by default.',
   'Your full GraphQL schema (incl. internal mutations) is readable by anyone.',
   'rule_ref',
   '{"rule":"graphqlIntrospection"}'::jsonb,
   'free',
   '{"_raw":"{ summary: \"Disable introspection in production and lock down the GraphQL endpoint.\", steps: [ \"Turn off introspection in your GraphQL server''s production config.\", \"Require authentication/authorization on the GraphQL endpoint.\", \"Disable any built-in GraphQL playground/IDE in production.\" ] }"}'::jsonb,
   'My production GraphQL endpoint allows introspection. Disable introspection and any GraphQL playground in production, and require auth on the endpoint. Show me the config for my server.',
   true),
  ('hidden-prompt-injection', 'generic', 'AI security', 'medium',
   'Hidden prompt-injection instructions in page HTML',
   'The page source embeds instructions for AI agents or scrapers using hidden HTML — comments, zero-opacity text, display:none blocks, data-system-prompt attributes, or zero-width Unicode. Untrusted page content can manipulate agents that summarize or act on the page.',
   'Hidden HTML tricks AI agents into leaking data or following attacker instructions — indirect prompt injection.',
   'rule_ref',
   '{"rule":"hiddenPromptInjection"}'::jsonb,
   'free',
   '{"summary":"Strip untrusted HTML before feeding it to AI agents; never trust page source as benign.","steps":["Sanitize or block-list HTML comments, hidden elements, and data-* prompt attributes before agent ingestion.","Detect zero-width Unicode and visually hidden text in content pipelines.","Treat all third-party page content as untrusted input to LLM prompts — use structured extraction, not raw HTML dumps."]}'::jsonb,
   'My site (or a page we ingest) has hidden prompt-injection vectors in the HTML source — comments, display:none instructions, data-system-prompt, or zero-width Unicode. Show how to strip these before any AI agent reads the page, and add detection in our content pipeline.',
   true),
  ('html-comment-credential-disclosure', 'generic', 'Information disclosure', 'high',
   'Database or admin credentials in HTML comment',
   'Page source contains an HTML comment disclosing database passwords, admin credentials, or SECRET staging keys readable by anyone viewing source.',
   'Credential hints in HTML comments leak secrets to unauthenticated visitors and search engine caches.',
   'rule_ref',
   '{"rule":"htmlCommentCredentialDisclosure"}'::jsonb,
   'deep',
   '{"summary":"Remove credential hints from HTML comments and rotate any disclosed secrets.","steps":["Delete HTML comments containing passwords or connection strings.","Rotate database credentials if they were ever committed to HTML.","Use server-side config files outside the web root for secrets."]}'::jsonb,
   'Our HTML includes a comment with database password hints. Show how to remove it and rotate credentials safely.',
   true),
  ('http-403-bypass-probe', 'generic', 'Broken access control', 'high',
   'HTTP 403 bypass via path normalization',
   'A protected admin or internal path returns 403 Forbidden, but path-normalization tricks (trailing slash, /./, ..;/, ;/) reach the resource without authentication.',
   'Attackers bypass access controls using URL encoding or path normalization to reach admin-only routes.',
   'rule_ref',
   '{"rule":"http403BypassProbe"}'::jsonb,
   'free',
   '{"summary":"Normalize and canonicalize request paths before authorization checks.","steps":["Reject or redirect ambiguous paths (trailing slash, dot segments, semicolon) before authz.","Apply authorization on the canonical path, not the raw request URI.","Return consistent 404 for unknown admin routes instead of distinguishable 403 responses."]}'::jsonb,
   'Our /admin route returns 403 but /admin/ bypasses it. Show how to canonicalize paths before authorization and block normalization bypasses.',
   true),
  ('idor-sequential-ids', 'generic', 'Broken access control', 'high',
   'Object access by predictable sequential IDs (IDOR)',
   'AI-generated CRUD scaffolding often exposes resources by sequential numeric IDs without an ownership check, so changing the ID in the URL returns another user''s data.',
   'Changing an ID in the URL may expose other users'' private records.',
   'rule_ref',
   '{"rule":"idorObjectAccess"}'::jsonb,
   'deep',
   '{"summary":"Add server-side ownership checks to every record lookup.","steps":["On every fetch/update/delete, verify the record belongs to the authenticated user.","Prefer non-guessable IDs (UUIDs) for user-facing resources.","Add automated tests that attempt cross-account access."]}'::jsonb,
   'My API may have IDOR: records are fetched by sequential ID without checking ownership. Add server-side ownership checks to every record access and switch user-facing IDs to UUIDs. Show the updated handlers.',
   true),
  ('inline-state-secrets', 'generic', 'Exposed secret', 'critical',
   'Server secrets embedded in the page''s inline app state',
   'Live server credentials are shipped inside the page''s inline JSON state (Next.js __NEXT_DATA__, or a window.__ENV__/__CONFIG__ bootstrap object). AI builders commonly pass server config straight into page props, so the secret is readable in View Source.',
   'A server key is sitting in your page''s HTML — visible in View Source.',
   'rule_ref',
   '{"rule":"inlineStateSecrets"}'::jsonb,
   'free',
   '{"summary":"Keep server secrets server-side; never pass them into client props or global state.","steps":["Remove secret values from getServerSideProps/getStaticProps output and any window.__ENV__ object.","Access server secrets only in server code (route handlers, server actions), never in props.","Rotate every credential that appeared in the page state."]}'::jsonb,
   'My page ships server secrets inside its inline app state (__NEXT_DATA__ / window.__ENV__). Move those values to server-only code so they never reach the client, and list the secrets I need to rotate.',
   true),
  ('insecure-cookies', 'generic', 'Security misconfiguration', 'medium',
   'Cookies set without the Secure flag',
   'One or more cookies are set over HTTPS without the Secure attribute, so the browser will also send them over plain HTTP where they can be intercepted. Session cookies additionally missing HttpOnly are readable by injected JavaScript.',
   'Session/auth cookies can leak over HTTP or be stolen via XSS.',
   'rule_ref',
   '{"rule":"insecureCookies"}'::jsonb,
   'free',
   '{"summary":"Set Secure, HttpOnly and SameSite on session/auth cookies.","steps":["Add the Secure attribute to every cookie so it is only sent over HTTPS.","Add HttpOnly to session/auth cookies so JavaScript cannot read them.","Set SameSite=Lax (or Strict) to reduce CSRF exposure."]}'::jsonb,
   'My site sets cookies without the Secure flag (and some without HttpOnly/SameSite). Update my cookie configuration so session/auth cookies are Secure, HttpOnly and SameSite=Lax, and show me the config.',
   true),
  ('lovable-client-only-auth', 'lovable', 'Broken access control', 'high',
   'Client-side-only route guarding (auth not enforced on data)',
   'Lovable scaffolds frequently gate UI routes in React but leave the Supabase/data layer open, so the API/DB can be hit directly without the UI ever loading.',
   'Hiding a page in the UI does not protect the data behind it; the data is still reachable.',
   'rule_ref',
   '{"rule":"clientOnlyAuth"}'::jsonb,
   'deep',
   '{"summary":"Enforce authorization at the data layer, not just the UI.","steps":["Add Row Level Security / server-side checks for every table and endpoint.","Treat client route guards as UX only, never as security."]}'::jsonb,
   'My app guards routes in the React UI but the underlying Supabase data is reachable without auth. Add server-side / RLS authorization on every table and endpoint so data is protected independently of the UI.',
   true),
  ('lovable-supabase-storage-public', 'lovable', 'Broken access control', 'high',
   'Lovable scaffold left a public Supabase Storage bucket',
   'Lovable templates default to a public avatars/uploads bucket. Without storage RLS, anyone with the anon key from your bundle can list and fetch objects.',
   'Public storage is a common Lovable default — user files may be world-readable.',
   'rule_ref',
   '{"rule":"supabaseStoragePublic"}'::jsonb,
   'free',
   '{"summary":"Lock down storage buckets and add RLS.","steps":["Set buckets to private.","Add storage.objects RLS.","Re-test with the anon key."]}'::jsonb,
   'My Lovable app has a public Supabase storage bucket. Make buckets private and add storage RLS policies so only the owning user can access their files.',
   true),
  ('missing-security-headers', 'generic', 'Security misconfiguration', 'medium',
   'Critical security headers missing',
   'Core security headers (Content-Security-Policy, X-Frame-Options, Strict-Transport-Security) are absent. Default Vite/Next templates produced by AI builders rarely set them.',
   'Missing headers leave you open to clickjacking and protocol-downgrade attacks.',
   'rule_ref',
   '{"rule":"securityHeaders"}'::jsonb,
   'free',
   '{"_raw":"{ summary: \"Add CSP, X-Frame-Options and HSTS to your responses.\", steps: [ \"Set Strict-Transport-Security with a long max-age.\", \"Set X-Frame-Options: DENY (or frame-ancestors in CSP).\", \"Add a Content-Security-Policy starting from a restrictive baseline.\" ] }"}'::jsonb,
   'My site is missing Content-Security-Policy, X-Frame-Options and Strict-Transport-Security headers. Add them in my framework config with sensible secure defaults and show me the config.',
   true),
  ('notices-file-lfi-probe', 'generic', 'Broken access control', 'critical',
   'Local file inclusion via file query parameter',
   'A notices or file-reader API accepts a file= parameter with path traversal sequences and returns /etc/passwd or similar system file contents.',
   'LFI via unsanitized file parameters enables reading sensitive server files and often leads to RCE.',
   'rule_ref',
   '{"rule":"noticesFileLfiProbe"}'::jsonb,
   'deep',
   '{"summary":"Never pass user input directly to filesystem reads; use allowlisted filenames.","steps":["Reject path traversal sequences (../, ..\\) in file parameters.","Use an allowlist of permitted notice filenames instead of arbitrary paths.","Run file-serving APIs with minimal filesystem permissions."]}'::jsonb,
   'Our /api/notices?file= endpoint returns /etc/passwd when given ../../../etc/passwd. Block path traversal and allowlist permitted filenames.',
   true),
  ('open-redirect-query-param', 'generic', 'Broken access control', 'medium',
   'Open redirect via query parameter',
   'A URL query parameter (e.g. ?next=, ?redirect=, ?return=) accepts an arbitrary external destination and the server issues an HTTP redirect to it. AI-generated login and OAuth flows commonly ship this pattern for post-auth navigation without validating the target.',
   'Attackers can craft links that redirect victims to phishing sites after visiting your domain.',
   'rule_ref',
   '{"rule":"openRedirect"}'::jsonb,
   'free',
   '{"summary":"Allow-list redirect destinations; never redirect to arbitrary URLs from query params.","steps":["Replace open redirects with an allow-list of same-origin paths (or known trusted hosts).","Reject absolute URLs unless the host is explicitly trusted.","Prefer relative paths only (e.g. /dashboard) for post-login navigation."]}'::jsonb,
   'My app has an open redirect: a query parameter like ?next= or ?redirect= sends users to arbitrary external URLs. Add an allow-list so only same-origin (or explicitly trusted) destinations are permitted, and show me the updated redirect handler.',
   true),
  ('openapi-debug-password-exposure', 'generic', 'Sensitive data exposure', 'critical',
   'OpenAPI debug endpoint exposes cleartext passwords',
   'An OpenAPI-documented GET debug route (e.g. /users/v1/_debug) returns user records with cleartext password fields without authentication.',
   'Debug API routes leak password hashes or cleartext credentials to unauthenticated callers.',
   'rule_ref',
   '{"rule":"openApiDebugPasswordExposure"}'::jsonb,
   'deep',
   '{"summary":"Remove debug endpoints from production; never return password fields in API responses.","steps":["Delete or disable /_debug and /debug/* routes outside development.","Strip password fields from all API serializers and OpenAPI schemas.","Require admin authentication for any diagnostic user-listing endpoints."]}'::jsonb,
   'Our API exposes GET /users/v1/_debug returning cleartext passwords without auth. Remove the debug route, strip password fields from responses, and show the hardened user serializer.',
   true),
  ('pentest-forge-linked-lab-probe', 'generic', 'Information disclosure', 'medium',
   'Public vulnerable lab catalog with linked benchmark targets',
   'Page catalogs PentestForge *.lab.pentest-forge.com vulnerable apps (Juice Shop, crAPI, VAmPI, Tomcat, WebLogic) or a linked subdomain returned unauthenticated vulnerable-app data.',
   'Public benchmark playground exposes live vulnerable targets — scanners and attackers can enumerate OWASP/API/CVE labs.',
   'rule_ref',
   '{"rule":"pentestForgeLinkedLabProbe"}'::jsonb,
   'deep',
   '{"summary":"Restrict lab hubs to authenticated testers; do not expose production-adjacent domains publicly.","steps":["Gate benchmark playgrounds behind auth or VPN.","Use robots/noindex and avoid linking live vulnerable apps from public marketing pages.","Isolate lab subnets from production networks."]}'::jsonb,
   'Our site links to public PentestForge vulnerable lab subdomains. Show how to restrict the catalog to authenticated users and network-isolate benchmark targets.',
   true),
  ('permissive-cors', 'generic', 'Security misconfiguration', 'medium',
   'CORS allows any origin',
   'The response sets Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *, letting any website read cross-origin responses. Combined with Allow-Credentials this exposes authenticated data to any site.',
   'Any website can read cross-origin responses from this endpoint.',
   'rule_ref',
   '{"rule":"permissiveCors"}'::jsonb,
   'free',
   '{"_raw":"{ summary: \"Replace the wildcard with an explicit allow-list of trusted origins.\", steps: [ \"Set Access-Control-Allow-Origin to specific trusted origins, not ''*''.\", \"Never combine a wildcard origin with Allow-Credentials: true.\", \"Reflect only origins from an allow-list you control.\" ] }"}'::jsonb,
   'My API returns Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *. Replace the wildcard CORS policy with an explicit allow-list of trusted origins and make sure credentials are never allowed with a wildcard. Show me the config.',
   true),
  ('php-strcmp-array-bypass', 'generic', 'Broken authentication', 'critical',
   'PHP strcmp bypass via array password parameter',
   'Live probe confirms PHP strcmp password check bypassed when password[] array is supplied — type juggling returns flag or success marker.',
   'GET password[]=1 bypasses strcmp auth and returns a CTF flag — critical authentication bypass.',
   'rule_ref',
   '{"rule":"phpStrcmpArrayBypass"}'::jsonb,
   'deep',
   '{"summary":"Reject non-string password input and use hash_equals for secret comparison.","steps":["Validate password is a string before strcmp or hash_equals.","Use hash_equals instead of strcmp for credential checks.","Return generic errors without leaking flags on failure."]}'::jsonb,
   'Our PHP login uses strcmp on $_GET password. Show how to block array type juggling and use hash_equals.',
   true),
  ('phpinfo-exposed', 'generic', 'Information disclosure', 'high',
   'PHP phpinfo() page reachable in production',
   'A phpinfo() diagnostic page is publicly reachable. It dumps PHP version, loaded extensions, server paths, and often environment variables — a goldmine for attackers planning further exploits.',
   'Anyone can read your full PHP/server configuration via phpinfo().',
   'rule_ref',
   '{"rule":"phpinfoExposed"}'::jsonb,
   'free',
   '{"summary":"Delete phpinfo pages from production and block the paths at the server.","steps":["Remove phpinfo.php / info.php from the deployed web root.","Return 404 for /phpinfo* paths in your server or WAF rules.","Rotate any secrets that may have appeared in phpinfo environment output."]}'::jsonb,
   'My production server still serves phpinfo.php (or similar). Remove those diagnostic files from the web root, block the paths in production, and show me the server config change.',
   true),
  ('phpmyadmin-exposed', 'generic', 'Information disclosure', 'high',
   'phpMyAdmin database admin login reachable',
   'A phpMyAdmin login page is publicly reachable. Database administration consoles expose schema browsing, SQL execution, and often default credentials on misconfigured hosts.',
   'Anyone can reach your phpMyAdmin login — a common path to full database compromise.',
   'rule_ref',
   '{"rule":"phpMyAdminExposed"}'::jsonb,
   'free',
   '{"summary":"Remove phpMyAdmin from public internet access and require VPN or IP allowlisting.","steps":["Block /phpMyAdmin and /phpmyadmin paths at the reverse proxy for non-admin networks.","Require strong authentication and MFA before any database admin console.","Use managed database consoles instead of exposing phpMyAdmin on app hosts."]}'::jsonb,
   'Production still serves phpMyAdmin on a public URL. Block admin database consoles from the internet and show me the server or WAF rule.',
   true),
  ('post-login-sql-bypass', 'generic', 'Injection', 'critical',
   'SQL injection bypasses POST login authentication',
   'A POST login form accepts SQL injection payloads in the username field, returning a session cookie and redirect to a protected dashboard — positive proof of authentication bypass via SQLi.',
   'Attackers can log in without valid credentials by injecting SQL into the login form.',
   'rule_ref',
   '{"rule":"postLoginSqlBypass"}'::jsonb,
   'deep',
   '{"summary":"Use parameterized queries for all authentication SQL; never concatenate user input.","steps":["Replace f-string or concatenated login queries with prepared statements.","Return generic login failure messages without SQL details.","Add rate limiting and monitoring on login endpoints."]}'::jsonb,
   'My Flask login accepts SQLi payloads like '' OR 1=1 -- and issues a session redirect. Switch to parameterized queries and show the fixed login handler.',
   true),
  ('postmessage-dom-xss-evidence', 'generic', 'Cross-site scripting', 'high',
   'DOM XSS via postMessage → innerHTML sink',
   'The shipped JavaScript registers a window message listener and assigns postMessage event data to innerHTML without sanitization. Third-party widgets (e.g. Gartner Peer Insights) have shipped this pattern — any page embedding the widget is vulnerable to DOM XSS from a malicious iframe.',
   'postMessage data is written to innerHTML — cross-origin iframe can execute script in the parent page.',
   'rule_ref',
   '{"rule":"postMessageDomXssEvidence"}'::jsonb,
   'deep',
   '{"summary":"Never assign postMessage data to innerHTML; sanitize or use textContent.","steps":["Replace innerHTML assignments from message handlers with textContent or a strict HTML sanitizer.","Validate event.origin against an explicit allow-list before processing message data.","Parse message payloads as JSON and reject HTML-bearing fields."]}'::jsonb,
   'My widget listens for postMessage and assigns event.data to innerHTML. Replace innerHTML with safe rendering (textContent or DOMPurify), validate event.origin, and show the fixed message handler.',
   true),
  ('redtiger-blind-login-form-evidence', 'generic', 'Injection', 'medium',
   'RedTiger blind login-bypass form disclosed',
   'RedTiger Hackit levels 2–10 expose password-only POST login forms (levelNlogin) for blind SQL injection and authentication bypass drills.',
   'Public RedTiger level pages document blind SQLi login-bypass training surfaces beyond the hub catalog.',
   'rule_ref',
   '{"rule":"redtigerBlindLoginFormEvidence"}'::jsonb,
   'deep',
   '{"summary":"RedTiger blind-login levels are intentional training — isolate hackit VMs from production.","steps":["Run RedTiger challenges only on dedicated training VMs.","Use parameterized queries for authentication in production.","Rate-limit blind login enumeration on public endpoints."]}'::jsonb,
   'We host RedTiger blind SQLi login-bypass levels. Show how to isolate training labs and fix authentication SQLi.',
   true),
  ('redtiger-classic-login-sql-evidence', 'generic', 'Injection', 'medium',
   'RedTiger classic SQLi login form disclosed',
   'RedTiger Hackit level1–2 expose username+password POST login forms with disclosed users table names for classic SQL injection drills.',
   'Public RedTiger level pages document classic SQLi login training surfaces with Tablename: levelN_users hints.',
   'rule_ref',
   '{"rule":"redtigerClassicLoginSqlEvidence"}'::jsonb,
   'deep',
   '{"summary":"RedTiger classic-login levels are intentional training — isolate hackit VMs from production.","steps":["Run RedTiger challenges only on dedicated training VMs.","Use parameterized queries for authentication in production.","Disable verbose table-name hints on public login pages."]}'::jsonb,
   'We host RedTiger classic SQLi login levels. Show how to isolate training labs and fix authentication SQLi.',
   true),
  ('redtiger-special-challenge-evidence', 'generic', 'Information disclosure', 'medium',
   'RedTiger special SMS exfiltration challenge disclosed',
   'RedTiger Hackit special1.php documents a non-SQL SMS exfiltration challenge with solution POST form — a web hacking drill beyond SQL injection levels.',
   'Public RedTiger special challenge pages document alternate web exploitation training surfaces outside the SQLi level catalog.',
   'rule_ref',
   '{"rule":"redtigerSpecialChallengeEvidence"}'::jsonb,
   'deep',
   '{"summary":"RedTiger special challenges are intentional training — isolate hackit VMs from production.","steps":["Run RedTiger challenges only on dedicated training VMs.","Do not expose SMS gateway or phone-number lookup endpoints without auth in production.","Rate-limit unauthenticated hackit enumeration."]}'::jsonb,
   'We host RedTiger-style special web hacking challenges. Show how to isolate training labs from production.',
   true),
  ('reflected-xss-query-param', 'generic', 'Cross-site scripting', 'high',
   'Reflected XSS via query parameter',
   'A search or filter query parameter is echoed unescaped in the HTML response body. Attackers can craft links that execute JavaScript in victims'' browsers when clicked.',
   'A URL parameter is reflected as raw HTML — session theft via crafted links is likely.',
   'rule_ref',
   '{"rule":"reflectedXss"}'::jsonb,
   'deep',
   '{"summary":"HTML-encode all user input before rendering; use framework auto-escaping.","steps":["Encode dynamic output in HTML contexts (use framework escaping, never raw concatenation).","Add a strict Content-Security-Policy without unsafe-inline.","Validate and reject HTML metacharacters on input where possible."]}'::jsonb,
   'My app reflects query parameters unescaped in HTML (reflected XSS). HTML-encode all dynamic output, remove raw string concatenation in templates, and add a strict CSP. Show the fixed handler/template.',
   true),
  ('replit-exposed-dot-replit', 'replit', 'Source exposure', 'medium',
   'Replit project config reachable over HTTP',
   'A deployed Replit app sometimes serves .replit or replit.nix, revealing run commands and internal layout.',
   'Project config files are downloadable from production.',
   'http_probe',
   '{"paths":["/.replit","/replit.nix","/.config/replit"],"expect":{"kind":"status_2xx_non_html"},"maxBytes":4096}'::jsonb,
   'free',
   '{"summary":"Stop serving Replit config files from the public web root.","steps":["Exclude .replit and nix configs from the static deploy.","Confirm they 404."]}'::jsonb,
   'My Replit deploy serves .replit or similar config files. Exclude them from the public web root and confirm they return 404.',
   true),
  ('replit-secrets-in-client', 'replit', 'Exposed secret', 'critical',
   'Replit secret referenced in client-side code',
   'Replit Secrets are meant for server-side use. AI-generated frontends often read process.env / import.meta.env in the browser, shipping those secrets to visitors.',
   'Secrets from Replit may be readable in the browser bundle.',
   'rule_ref',
   '{"rule":"publicEnvPrefixLeak"}'::jsonb,
   'free',
   '{"summary":"Use Replit Secrets only in backend/server code.","steps":["Move secret reads into Express/FastAPI/server routes.","Rotate any secret that appeared in client JS."]}'::jsonb,
   'My Replit app may be exposing Secrets in client-side code. Find every client reference to secrets/env vars, move them server-side, and list what I must rotate.',
   true),
  ('robots-sensitive-paths', 'generic', 'Information disclosure', 'medium',
   'robots.txt discloses sensitive paths',
   'The published robots.txt file lists Disallow paths that reveal admin panels, backups, or internal routes — a free map for attackers.',
   'robots.txt advertises admin/backup paths attackers can probe.',
   'rule_ref',
   '{"rule":"robotsSensitivePaths"}'::jsonb,
   'free',
   '{"_raw":"{ summary: \"Remove sensitive paths from robots.txt or block them at the edge.\", steps: [ \"Delete Disallow entries that name admin, backup, or config paths.\", \"Protect sensitive routes with auth regardless of robots.txt.\", \"Use noindex meta tags for pages you don''t want indexed instead of advertising paths.\" ] }"}'::jsonb,
   'My robots.txt lists sensitive paths like /admin or /backup. Rewrite it to avoid disclosing internal routes, and show me how to protect those paths with auth instead.',
   true),
